Jack Bolsen wrote:
> I've played for about seven years and I still don't know why I love this
> game, except that it's a lot like Dungeons and Dragons and there is
> always something new to do, even after multiple ascensions.

1. The infinite variety.

2. The intellectual challenge.

3. The sense of achievement which comes from a discovery or a success.

4. The tempo of the game (ie, player controls the pace of turns and one
can wait and think)

5. The visual style and user interface, a constant reminder that less
is more.

6. Playing solo, neither advantaged nor handicapped by anyone else.

7. The extent of resource material.

8. This group.

So there's eight reasons, but really the question is like asking
someone "Why do you love football?" - the main reason is familiarity,
habit and enjoyment.  I have played Nethack for over 15 years and,
given sufficient spare time to sit down at the computer, I keep coming
back to it for much the same reason as people keep climbing Everest.

